Hanover's idyllic old town the city  wants to make sure its visitors and  tourists don't miss a single site or  attraction that's why they came up with  this little red line  it runs 4 kilometers through the city  guiding you to 36 of its most important  sites including plenty of examples of  Hanover's royal splendor this city was a  seat of royal power for more than 200  years home to the later Kings of Hanover  from one of Europe's oldest noble  families this was supposed to be their  new palace but the king of Hanover was  stripped of power by the Prussians in  1866 and never got to move in instead  the palace remained empty for ten years  until a university took over the  building  and overs Opera House is another example  of royal architecture the king wanted to  locate the building in what was then the  city center and the architects had to  fight hard to get their project on its  outskirts approved but in the end  Hannover grew and developed around the  Opera House Hannover continued to grow  in size and splendor under Prussian rule  - with buildings like the new city hall  today it's become a landmark curious  visitors can take a tour exploring its  interior all the way up to the roof from  the dome visitors have a tremendous  panoramic view of Hanover  right in the heart of the city the  man-made Mashti lake is one of Hanover  as many green oasis  and so were the banks of the liner River  the spacious Gorge garden is a landscape  park created in the English style and  it's a favorite among students  visitors taking a stroll for the Royal  Gardens are transported back to the 17th  century The Grocer garden or great  garden was the centerpiece of the  electors summer residence it was  envisioned and realized by Electress  Sophia of Hanover who wanted to create a  sort of ballroom outdoors the central  focus of the garden is this fountain  which shoots over 80 meters into the sky  for both locals and visitors picnicking  at the royal residence is a special  treat and don't leave the Royal Gardens  without seeing the famous grotto  designed by Nicky to some five here the  artists created a magical world made of  colorful mosaics its accents like this  that revealed a modern face of Hanover  was controversial bananas sculptures are  now an integral part of the city Hanover  has been investing in open-air modern  art since the 1970s and some of it ends  up in the city's pedestrian zones even  an overs bus stops have a special Flair  some were designed by international  artists  of course there's art indoors too like  in the August Kastner Museum the  building itself is an important  attraction for art lovers the block like  structure is wrapped in walls of  concrete latticework and covered with  five thousand windows inside visitors  find a range of design objects  the Sprenger museum focuses on  contemporary and classic art  a world-renowned Museum it features up  to 25 different exhibitions every year  whether taking in the cultural  attractions or relaxing outdoors in its  parks and gardens Hanover is certain to  delight
 

